<h2 id="task">First the task, then the format</h2><p>The landing page and the website do not compete with each other. These are different tools within the same system. The first helps to quickly focus attention on a specific proposal. The second collects trust, assortment, content and several appeal scenarios.</p><p>The mistake begins when the format is chosen out of habit: “we need a website because it’s more solid” or “we’ll make a landing page because it’s faster.” In both cases, you can end up with a beautiful page that doesn't help sales.</p><h2 id="landing">When a landing page is enough</h2><p>Landing is justified if the project has one clear entrance: the launch of a residential complex, a separate service, an advertising campaign or a test of a new direction. It helps you quickly connect the message, advertisement and contact form.</p><ul><li>One key sentence and one audience.</li><li>Limited advertising campaign with clear metrics.</li><li>You need to quickly check demand and offer.</li></ul><figure><img src="/assets/new_google_advertising_example.png" alt="Example of an advertising interface"><figcaption>A landing page is especially effective when it has a specific advertising script behind it.</figcaption></figure><h2 id="site">When you need a website</h2><p>A website is needed when the user chooses between several objects, formats or services. Structure, filtering, evidence, SEO pages and a consistent path from first visit to conversion are important here.</p><p>This doesn't mean the site has to be big from day one. It is important to lay down the architecture: sections, page types, analytics and the ability to expand without rebuilding the entire product.</p><h3>A website is not a replacement for an advertising campaign</h3><p>Even a strong website does not create demand on its own. But it makes traffic clearer: it allows you to see what interests the user, which pages lead to an application, and where the process loses a person.</p><h2 id="start">Where to start the project</h2><p>Describe the offer, audience, acquisition channels and the action the user should take. After this, it becomes clear whether you need a first landing page, a compact website, or a full-fledged page system.</p>
